1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Inga Marie and her mother Margrethe arrived in New York City on May 16th,1906. Inga writes "We passed the Statue of Liberty. It stood there like bidding us all welcome. It surely helped". 
2 CONT
2 CONT When the White Star Oceanic docked at Ellis Island, only First Class passengers were allowed to get off the ship. "We were fenced off. It is so hot." On May 17th, Syttende Mai, "We can not leave the ship today either. There came s
2 CONC o many ships before us, and all had to go through Ellis Island." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Inga and Margrethe were processed through Ellis Island on May 18, 1906. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then they were on a train headed for Baron Wisconsin, were Mr. Andersen, who had sponsored them and paid for their tickets, lived. Upon their arrival he told Inga and Margrethe he had sold his farm and was moving to Silverton Oreg
2 CONC on. They were welcome to go too, so they did. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Inga met Thorbjorn, or Tom as he was called, at a Norwegian church picnic. He was the President of the Young Peoples Society. She noticed that she had lost her purse with her first American dollar in it, just about the time Tom wa
2 CONC s announcing to the crowd that a purse had been found. Shy and with a red face, Inga went forward to get her purse from the man she would marry in October the following year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 41 years of marriage, 7 children, 20 grandchildren, the Great depression, the WPA, years of cooking, a return to her Norway, constant service to others, and in the end she writes - My motto: Give me Dear Lord my daily bread, and h
2 CONC eaven when I am dead. That is all I need. 
2 CONT
2 CONT And we say - Takk for alt.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
