2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Ida and her hardanger 1986
2 NOTE Ida Lee was known as the Hardanger Lady.  She taught many people the beautiful Norwegian stitchery at her own dining room table.  Her handwork won many blue ribbons at state county fairs, was displayed in museums and at festivals,
3 CONC  and a piece she designed won third place in a nation-wide contest.  It also appears in a Hardanger pattern book.
3 CONT The framed piece in this picture is a sampler, or sample of many Hardanger stitches.  It toured in an art exhibit, and spent six months at the Smithsonian Museum in Washington DC.
3 CONT In the October 1988 issue of the Sons of Norway Viking magazine, 
3 CONT there is an article - A Heritage of Handwork.  It begins, "Ida Lee's heritage is in handwork, but she'll leave her own legacy in lace".
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p6/4966/3456/53444836ad022c9d/1919_original.jpg?hash=403ca8875869eb6e5f4da08e044215ff95550e5f8cd4e68b76cfd115e6c05677.1795161599
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Hanson Family - 1919
2 NOTE Inga writes - It seems like all my children developed a habit of swallowing things.  When Ruth was just crawling around on the floor, she found the bottom part of a collar button.  It was a round disk and sharp.  When I got to her
3 CONC  and looked in her mouth, it just shone gold but closed up so she could not breathe at all.  I knew I had no time to call the doctor, but one of the kids ran for Dad.  She just could not get it down, it had to come up.  So I held 
3 CONC her with one hand, and jammed my finger down her throat as far as I could.  She gagged, and up it came.
3 CONT I held the baby, got the disk from the floor, and put it in the stove so I would not see it anymore.  Then I sat down and held my crying baby, held her close and cried with her.  By then my husband and all the children came runnin
3 CONC g in.  Thanks be to God, it was over this time too.
1 OBJE
